{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"","type":"thinking"},{"text":"Good eye on both. Answers:\n\n## `arg_entries` naming\n\nIt's **not** the ArgTree — it's the ArgTree's **top-level entries** (the name→oid map). SPEC is clear that an ArgTree is \"a git tree containing named args,\" and in this scope the whole ArgTree is already present as `arg_tree: &str` — its **hash**, the cache key. So there are two distinct things:\n\n- `arg_tree` — the ArgTree (its git-tree hash),\n- `arg_entries` — that tree's top-level name→oid entries, which is all the reader superset-match compares against (the match never looks at nested subtrees).\n\nRenaming `arg_entries` → `arg_tree` would actually *reduce* consistency: it'd collide with the hash that's already named `arg_tree`, and conflate \"the ArgTree\" with \"its top level.\" And `arg_entries` is exactly what the rest of the code already calls this map — `runner::Job.arg_entries` (whose type is even aliased `ArgTree`), and `compute::args_entries`. So the name is the consistent choice; what was missing was making the *relationship* explicit. Let me tighten the doc so it reads in ArgTree terms:","type":"text"},{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01JP8Mu7z16a12vQKE6uSKF6","input":{"file_path":"crates/server/src/secrets.rs","new_string":"/// The secrets visible to a job whose ArgTree's top-level entries are\n/// `arg_entries` (name → oid, as `compute::args_entries` reads them) and whose\n/// std tree is `std`: every secret with at least one reader whose resolved\n/// partial arg tree is a subset of those entries. Deduped by name.","old_string":"/// The secrets visible to a job whose top-level arg entries are `arg_entries`\n/// and whose std tree is `std`: every secret with at least one reader whose\n/// resolved partial arg tree is a subset of `arg_entries`. Deduped by name."},"name":"edit","type":"tool_use"}],"results":[{"content":[{"text":"exit: 0\nstdout:\n4:- an ArgTree: a git tree containing named args:\n14:WorkRequest contains ArgTree, stack, trace id, etc #todo\n15:- The ArgTree is the cache key\n17:We generally talk about ArgTrees, not images. An image is just one arg (see\n18:below), so one simple ArgTree is one that only contains an image; richer\n19:ArgTrees carry other args alongside it. Passing around ArgTrees rather than\n24:# Forming an ArgTree\n26:The simplest ArgTree is one that only specifies an image\n46:Currying takes an ArgTree and args and returns a new ArgTree, binding the existing args and the new args\n52:When caos gets a WorkRequest, it builds the image specified by the ArgTree. When doing this, it adds a few pieces to the image:\n74:- It caches work based on the ArgTree. The same work is never run a second time\n106:    - Rust can fetch dependencies just based on a cargo file, but it won't compile them without top-level sources. This is the difference between roughly 3 seconds and 12 seconds to compile cold. Go doesn't have this problem\n126:    - WorkRequests as described below. The input is the hash of the ArgTree as a git tree (the stack and trace id travel alongside it, not inside the hashed tree). The result is the hash of the WorkResult\n143:hand. Both build the same ArgTree, so a tool cannot behave differently\n184:- Args are part of the ArgTree, and the ArgTree is the cache key. The same\n197:- any other tree — its top-level listing is shown\n\nstderr:\n","type":"text"}],"tool_use_id":"toolu_017MRC5PNS3Rhaz8KBDgMfSq","type":"tool_result"}],"v":1}